sweet-fb-fifo (lowclk 2026-11-15, research 166 levers 1-3): swap FIFO, completion-kicked gate and a per-second presents row for the panel copy of AppleCLCD (iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp) and a kick hook in the Mali completion thread (bsd/dev/sweet_mali.c, sweet_mali.h). CODE ONLY: no register access, no new hardware write, no firmware command, no bus store; the panel copy is the same CPU row memcpy into the same panel memory as before. The new write path is unreviewed (AGENTS rule 8 is not triggered by software queueing, the driver decides). Boot-args default off = behaviour unchanged (only never-taken branches, new members, one extra loop over four NULL pointers in present()).
Boot-arg sweetfb_fifo=N (0 or absent = latest wins as today; 1..254 = FIFO capped at N standing ticks; 255 = uncapped; any other value = GAP line, off): swap() used to overwrite the single pending record (fPending), so a 2nd swap before the tick replaced the 1st, which was never shown [read from research 166 s.1.1, SF swap()]. Now the head (fPending) stays and the 2nd swap waits in one HOLD slot (fHold, fHoldSeq) for the next tick (counted held); a 3rd swap replaces the HOLD (latest wins inside the second slot, counted replaced and fifo_full). The tick takes the head (the gate tests the head's seqno as before); after a present that leaves a HOLD, the HOLD becomes the head and `standing` counts that tick. Cap: with N < 255, when standing >= N and the HOLD is GPU-complete (seq_done >= fHoldSeq, or no gate, or Mali unavailable = fail open) the head is dropped and the HOLD is shown instead (counted catchup and replaced), so the added latency is bounded by N ticks. The queue logic is the pure block SFQ-PURE-BEGIN/END (sfq_arrive, sfq_catchup, sfq_take, sfq_row_take), host-tested by tools/test_sweet_fifo368.py with mutants.
Surface lifetime [read from source]: a swap record holds only surface IDs; present() used to look them up in the surface table at copy time (sweet_surface_copy: retain under the table lock; the table entry is dropped when the last user goes, SweetSurface.cpp:195-215). With sweetfb_fifo >= 1, swap() takes that reference on every masked layer surface of a record it queues (head or HOLD), moves it with the record (promoteHold, takeHead), releases it when the record is replaced or dropped (after the unlock) and hands it to present() through fPresentSurf, which surfFor() checks before the table. So a held swap's surface object, memory and kernel mapping stay valid until its copy is done, even when CoreAnimation destroyed the IOSurface meanwhile. LIMIT, said plainly: a reference keeps the memory alive, it does not stop CoreAnimation from drawing into a reused buffer while the swap waits (SwapWait returns at once and nothing tells CA when a swap was shown, today's rule), so a swap held one tick longer than before can show newer pixels than it was swapped with; the 0361 skip rule and the SWEET FPS hash see what is really copied. Default off keeps the old window (one tick).
Boot-arg sweetfb_gatekick=1 (0/absent off; needs sweetfb_gate=1 and sweetfb_hz >= 60, else a GAP line and off): when the gate defers the head at a tick because the GPU is still busy (research 166 point 2: 1.0-1.2/s), the tick arms the deferred seqno in Mali (sweet_mali_kick_arm). sm_thread calls sm_kick_tick() after each loop pass, after its unlock; when seq_done >= the armed seqno, exactly one of the arm and the hook fires (seq_cst store/fence/load pairs + compare-exchange, see the comment above sm_kick_tick) the registered function sweet_fb_kick_cb, which enters a one-shot IOTimerEventSource with timeout 0 on the fb workloop. kickFired() then copies the head once if nothing was copied since the last tick (fLastPresentCount < the tick count, so still at most one copy per tick interval), the tick deferred it (fDeferred) and seq_done really passed it (otherwise counted stale or notdone and re-armed). No poll loop, no new timer period; Mali not up / dead = arm returns -1, GAP line, the next tick copies as before.
Row (once per second while something flows; on with sweetfb_copytime=1, sweetfb_drawn=1, sweetfb_fifo or sweetfb_gatekick): `SWEET FB: presents P drawn D held H replaced R gatekick K fifo_full F`. presents = panel copies made (tick + kick, counted when the head is taken, the same events as the `panel copy` total); drawn = presents whose swap had MORE than 4 GPU jobs since the previous swap (jobs = Mali submitted seqno at this swap minus at the previous swap, the rule of tools/fps-wholerun JOBS_DRAWN and SWEET SWAPT; unknown seqno = not drawn; a redraw without a swap = not drawn); held = swaps put in the HOLD; replaced = swaps discarded unshown (overwritten head, overwritten HOLD, catchup drops); gatekick = presents made by the kick; fifo_full = swaps that found the HOLD full. Invariant (tested): swaps = presents + replaced + still queued. With sweetfb_fifo also `SWEET FB: fifo cap N catchup C standing S`; with sweetfb_gatekick also `SWEET FB: gatekick armed A fired F stale S notdone N unavail U`. Which count D1 means (presents P or drawn D) is the user's call (research 166 s.5). The SWAPT, PACE, GATE and `panel copy` row formats are not touched.
Rule 7: unknown or unusable boot-arg values, a refused arm, a failed allocation and a missing hook are GAP lines; every fallback has a counter (replaced, fifo_full, catchup, stale, notdone, unavail). Not run on hardware.
